#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3 - x^2$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=px+q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the values of $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^3 - x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2})=3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1, 0){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635 :topic/math-academy-id 418 :topic/title "Equations of Lines in Point-Slope Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3826d54-f39e-5e45-982c-0196c96b30c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090533} {:answer/id #uuid "bf822091-623a-5b75-a700-06562c3fa7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090534} {:answer/id #uuid "c014a862-aa95-5ee5-8cc4-90a1115cf633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090535} {:answer/id #uuid "e535f72e-5b43-5878-a978-9a20e1d8470d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090536} {:answer/id #uuid "48a27df3-0a04-52a4-9003-fd2c61c07e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090537}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7907bce1-6e91-548c-bf8c-f50341390d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090532}] :question/id #uuid "2a649b33-83ae-5424-9852-7d09d082fffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-47406" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^{2}$ at the point $(2, 8)$ is given by $x + py = q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p + q$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355be2d9-4b88-5b77-9339-b936d34c03d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327396} {:answer/id #uuid "464056c0-a0f2-5bfe-b408-9aa941897b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327549} {:answer/id #uuid "877dfb13-75fb-5a2c-9893-61ca31652033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328100} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e2320c-32e8-55f4-858e-5dbe27b873eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328534} {:answer/id #uuid "beaa42aa-dbd3-5b01-b81b-d37b04fb64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "464056c0-a0f2-5bfe-b408-9aa941897b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327549} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf413bf-6c88-5e40-a13e-f1edd13516cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329596}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1cac491-67f0-58ca-a542-a3096fac9ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47408" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{5}-2x$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=ax+b$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-2x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-2x)=10{x}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=10(1)^{4}-2=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f08372b9-b9c4-55c6-8130-e0b8b3702f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08372b9-b9c4-55c6-8130-e0b8b3702f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232834} :answer-field/id #uuid "db38cb75-4a2a-5faa-9624-427353b91e8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b547da1-c70a-5896-a34a-6ec75b964807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b547da1-c70a-5896-a34a-6ec75b964807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232836} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6f2a88-c9b9-5995-986d-d685474cd66d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a4f8ec-9201-5937-9164-8f60e8aa0551" :question/math-academy-id "q-340942" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ at the point $(2,9).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+1)=4x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,9):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=4(2)=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-72 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8\\cdot y & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040d200c-28e8-5295-aed7-d520e9cd3e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "040d200c-28e8-5295-aed7-d520e9cd3e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232867} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbf44b5-3a8a-56c7-b5b8-1662b361ece7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cd55b4-a689-56b0-ad87-45efc20ee812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03cd55b4-a689-56b0-ad87-45efc20ee812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232869} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed007c73-3599-5c78-af4d-81875dcfb067" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31017300-66b4-5dbf-aba1-d07ddfcf96a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342163" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-{x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,3).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4x-{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-{x}^{3})=4-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=4-3({1}^{2})=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-1\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+1\\cdot y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234077 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a936e865-c9ea-5dbc-8fe2-541cf4f4a61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47405" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,16)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})=6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)^{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(16) & =-\\frac{1}{24}(x-(2)) \\\\ 24y-384 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+24y & =386.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=386-24=362.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1ba0de-62d4-5250-bb47-4f2a59dfcd01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1ba0de-62d4-5250-bb47-4f2a59dfcd01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234782} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c9641fb-c8f3-5eae-84db-0c0deb61bedb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235032}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dde111b-f4d3-5f08-91c6-efd9593de57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167327" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4decc47b-808c-50a3-a38e-a4db1547ccf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4decc47b-808c-50a3-a38e-a4db1547ccf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234665} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a661154-3b70-50d8-a6af-d295b9a56bb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d970612-b8f0-51a2-9d52-e3fa3b8f917e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167781" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc92670-1ee0-5a94-8633-ae4dbac0d967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236503} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4214d-961d-587a-99f1-77840683d122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236888} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b8f9a0-77e0-5918-ba96-c076ccd0a4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237149} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b35714-a77b-5cf7-b620-bbbde6e0b91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237187} {:answer/id #uuid "f71cabe8-d7a8-53fa-97ce-f3e2f21c3251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4214d-961d-587a-99f1-77840683d122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236888} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc67540-c6a4-5c72-ab19-c7578f38fabe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf6fcd75-6ae3-55a9-acc1-22b5660350be" :question/math-academy-id "q-103546" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ 2y-8 & =x-1 \\\\ x-2y & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=-7-(-2)=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8aacf8a-923a-5706-a78c-108e65099a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8aacf8a-923a-5706-a78c-108e65099a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313826} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ac9af9-f5be-5aac-9b98-a73bb56de950" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f398213-9b43-5009-a712-5e39a192aab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f398213-9b43-5009-a712-5e39a192aab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312352} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e679c8-0512-5ea7-a995-5f08819c8352" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6db0058b-56f3-5a30-bae9-0cf86c420390" :question/math-academy-id "q-340945" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(2,2).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-16{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{16}{(2)^{3}}=-\\frac{16}{8}=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-4 & =x-2 \\\\ x-2\\cdot y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8672281f-8981-5889-ae04-fc0ebe51ce35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8672281f-8981-5889-ae04-fc0ebe51ce35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315875} :answer-field/id #uuid "076abe3c-2890-583c-ace8-ff9d99106670" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a195177d-1813-5a16-ae86-c4c7cd7b18a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a195177d-1813-5a16-ae86-c4c7cd7b18a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316028} :answer-field/id #uuid "9edf45fe-a749-5a58-b21a-dc03a59337f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316914}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7217f3b7-79ba-5f9c-b811-2d79787fbad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342162" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=6{x}^{2}-7x+4$ at the point $(0,4).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-7x+4.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-7x+4)=12x-7\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,4):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=12(0)-7=-7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{7}(x-0) \\\\ 7(y-4) & =x \\\\ 7y-28 & =x \\\\ x-7\\cdot y & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14739741-a199-5044-95db-68e615cfe350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14739741-a199-5044-95db-68e615cfe350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315242} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c1804f-c3c3-527f-ad52-389c7f415fc7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef369bbf-3dd9-544a-b45c-a35adc1c8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef369bbf-3dd9-544a-b45c-a35adc1c8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5cbe1d4-2ba5-517a-85ba-01b462393fb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316932}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "225f0ba1-57bb-5f34-91e4-9ca304d64d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342168" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,-6).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eaf6866-59c7-5bcf-85b0-5e039d9cf80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eaf6866-59c7-5bcf-85b0-5e039d9cf80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315568} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee849c8-86a4-5fad-acfb-56e96048a591" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901ca621-ed5f-5750-a913-063b18972900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "901ca621-ed5f-5750-a913-063b18972900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315924} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a60e86-2a15-5a46-a72a-b8277699810a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316981}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "773759d8-9f76-5cef-8ffc-9fa90fd16743" :question/math-academy-id "q-342190" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16(y-\\frac{3}{4}) & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e1490f-8a2f-5aa1-b46d-abffe2ecf67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327216} {:answer/id #uuid "2a251a8f-d542-5932-929c-cdccb0e5eaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327299} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2eccf0-2aa7-5272-b036-0190d7bed899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328001} {:answer/id #uuid "906c08ad-b6aa-508c-9085-351bcf96badc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328194} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f5f70f-61df-5d3d-b04a-f2eee1395813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a251a8f-d542-5932-929c-cdccb0e5eaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327299} :answer-field/id #uuid "24cc6f6b-5575-52a5-a07a-afc26dd7d423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abc350e7-3687-5b2a-9ff1-fa33c7a23d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-47409" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-2}-x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{-3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-8(1)^{-3}-1=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10131252-4695-56ed-8c59-747e545f594c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392584} {:answer/id #uuid "e6984861-e3cb-5365-9e9e-1ecd4311dcb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392585} {:answer/id #uuid "859687f9-c59b-551f-aaa5-e7e3c04ba6c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392586} {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} {:answer/id #uuid "41016988-d005-506a-b687-2940e1c88cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9549a2f-75a6-5fa7-b3e3-19c27b3b484f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "414ee72f-747c-573a-9cad-a9cbdad6101a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47410" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(7) & =-\\frac{1}{10}(x-1) \\\\ 10y-70 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+10y & =71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=10+71=81.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190621cf-ca9e-5595-8816-3501bc000442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-\\pi}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190621cf-ca9e-5595-8816-3501bc000442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-\\pi}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513597} :answer-field/id #uuid "059506ee-d597-56af-bfce-bf17819d6958" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514211}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "745a29fd-db69-5896-9d5f-b9b3dcaa95b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-166074" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 4, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091953 :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-148" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^3 -x^2$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = 2x^3-x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x)  =6x^2 -2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 6(1)^2 - 2(1) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac 1 4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473 :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711 :topic/math-academy-id 332 :topic/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb507b99-cb17-5881-9f7c-7f3bdb48c8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090539} {:answer/id #uuid "f06d2f10-e5b1-5029-99a8-6df6c910ab6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090540} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a0ae91-abfc-541f-9457-17bb6c55ce47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090541} {:answer/id #uuid "27e35489-630e-5eed-b771-9a6e6cae4ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090542} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6b3f52-851a-594f-823b-749c8f9ccf15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090543}] :answer-field/id #uuid "47454a3f-e62a-5e62-92e7-aabf4470cebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090538}] :question/id #uuid "f6abbd38-d6c1-583b-be9d-3b05f3174640" :question/math-academy-id "q-47407" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = \\sqrt{x} - 2$ at the point $(1,-1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adad1e21-46cd-56cb-9d6f-f36d5bce72b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090551} {:answer/id #uuid "cea18fcd-e3f3-500a-8bc0-2225e0cc5671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090552} {:answer/id #uuid "d7743646-f07f-5af5-b590-80e44330787e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090553} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe51417-bb73-5d71-a36c-0efa5a7bc0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090554} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddd056c-7bd7-5062-99ef-1d27d0c50cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090555}] :answer-field/id #uuid "26ab7884-b472-5138-8736-d4998552be0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090550}] :question/id #uuid "c58b3f22-e629-5123-ba92-7e9d9ea02ad2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47413" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 3x^{2} - 2x$ at the point $(2, 8)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f37ef0f4-6e25-5f13-af31-6202e980e423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37ef0f4-6e25-5f13-af31-6202e980e423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232532} :answer-field/id #uuid "a26800fe-c174-5f19-a271-3c1a5bd102ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232531}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "669f8ad9-dff7-5df9-8932-67ecc42dc84c" :question/math-academy-id "q-308798" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+3x)=2x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=2(-1)+3=-2+3=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33aed63a-b507-5f47-be6d-1ea1968fc56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33aed63a-b507-5f47-be6d-1ea1968fc56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232861} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a34980-8785-53b7-9d2d-c2704de01739" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232860}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7478ba97-dd0a-53c5-98ba-0b472abb594d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342142" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-3{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-3{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-3{x}^{2})=-6x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=-6(1)=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7785e0f8-d36d-58f1-b4bb-92dad6d81157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7785e0f8-d36d-58f1-b4bb-92dad6d81157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232863} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed26559a-60f3-5110-9c11-8fb12d780195" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232862}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31e569a8-362b-51d8-8186-4764daa7d587" :question/math-academy-id "q-342147" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,-8).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2})=\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4}-8x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-8):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=\\frac{5}{4}({2}^{4})-8(2)=20-16=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f91eef-b83d-531e-8528-82c356da256b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f91eef-b83d-531e-8528-82c356da256b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232865} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4685f24-ff78-51cf-b109-1a2be8dffda8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232864}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff198fc0-46a3-5bea-900f-f995439b9d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342152" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ at the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=6{(-1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{(-1)^{5}}=6-4=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234307 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0de752-0812-5082-ba75-5deee887ec8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71525" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-x+3$ at the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-x+3.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-x+3)=6{x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(1)=6(1)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0dfaf6-4b26-5780-916c-6b6e7cc84e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236012} {:answer/id #uuid "a04d6a2e-4584-529d-a61d-427012951a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236818} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ea2eb4-6e83-5f3b-a04e-b53fcabdb24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237145} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e93d2c-4a24-59ba-9508-adf45fe9d13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237231} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfbd345-bc08-5590-b695-612eea6c6693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1e93d2c-4a24-59ba-9508-adf45fe9d13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237231} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fea9d47-2732-52fb-98f5-f254a1901480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27376e81-fcda-5685-8dbf-1c9048989040" :question/math-academy-id "q-103531" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2}-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{1/2}-4(1)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e61460f-455d-520d-93b5-dffbbc89788f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236320} {:answer/id #uuid "69eb4aac-b14f-57bd-a480-1ab582de3ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236485} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3b9c84-9c83-5f6f-86e3-c86711996c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236519} {:answer/id #uuid "86d05e0e-a4ab-5588-b9b4-148c9e0ee1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236656} {:answer/id #uuid "cb23d69f-8e38-59d2-a2f7-6d29f4a5143d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb23d69f-8e38-59d2-a2f7-6d29f4a5143d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237106} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9fd797f-3553-5404-8639-7c51e487bdfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237766}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64a70883-2f1e-5226-9a93-f2c233eeb60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103532" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}$ at the point $(1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-4\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{2}-10(1)^{3/2}=-7\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line  ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de724df-8b1c-5535-8e44-daf76049ece3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235891} {:answer/id #uuid "23b48bbe-ffa7-54a8-99f9-dc3950946ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236047} {:answer/id #uuid "4e607467-80e3-56bc-b439-ef24f59e6ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236319} {:answer/id #uuid "b66f9765-eff6-521c-bc5e-6e7d22591dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236972} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4263ad-8b05-53b5-bdc9-5ccb768cb0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e607467-80e3-56bc-b439-ef24f59e6ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236319} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fef744c-c73f-546c-b977-0ab52629ab8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "602176f6-b1e0-573b-b42a-73b7532c22d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-103543" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x$ at the point $(1,9).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-5}+7x) \\\\  & =-10{x}^{-6}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=-10(1)^{-6}+7=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ba581c-ebc6-55ea-99ee-e980b997d941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67ba581c-ebc6-55ea-99ee-e980b997d941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea3ff45-b371-5b5e-8ab3-a1c221cf4178" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317154}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cc6d36c-e26a-5fcd-bcb0-d88e0fe39c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-342153" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2).$\n\n${m}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a6c80-4207-50f6-8a34-1d93a5f89fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327287} {:answer/id #uuid "307e57c3-1572-5e33-a5f9-3472b90bf91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327363} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ee7da7-a08a-5f34-86ea-f3f106a5f98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328912} {:answer/id #uuid "e33c1589-993a-5e15-9743-78a5a9f95009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328926} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c839b-8755-5fd8-b8d8-b4929a06d14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9c839b-8755-5fd8-b8d8-b4929a06d14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329142} :answer-field/id #uuid "197f2e71-4c00-5a81-830b-269b6b29fd90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329228}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "704dafc6-9839-55e3-9683-dc5e275270d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47449" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105a0ee2-ecfe-5f6b-abf3-c2b8990ff5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336186} {:answer/id #uuid "8afbf511-93b1-574c-aea0-f719ed534072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337288} {:answer/id #uuid "9b55a9ee-2b2e-5710-b96a-5529ce814f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337440} {:answer/id #uuid "b83a05a7-995d-5078-bf9a-4b58abb10a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337665} {:answer/id #uuid "e2cfabde-266f-53a5-a5bf-2a8c9659561a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105a0ee2-ecfe-5f6b-abf3-c2b8990ff5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336186} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb39a159-1af8-51a3-9955-b31d00f03bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338721}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b93f0c3-f4cf-5391-8374-d4b6970c54ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71524" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}-5$ at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-5.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-5)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091954 :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x} -4x$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = (1)^{-1/2} - 4 = -3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = \\dfrac 1 3.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(7,0).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394 :topic/math-academy-id 398 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17dbb435-0cbf-53b0-b774-95a09e5dd5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241384} {:answer/id #uuid "52de3f96-3021-5e86-a521-dd5eaf2b4c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241918} {:answer/id #uuid "7aaf9455-fc42-5674-a140-8638e322d2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242258} {:answer/id #uuid "8b234896-b4c5-57ed-a626-2274af12bce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242411} {:answer/id #uuid "aecf5e6b-c2d8-5faf-a09d-6ece73a85a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{16}{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b234896-b4c5-57ed-a626-2274af12bce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242411} :answer-field/id #uuid "562461af-5947-5911-b5fb-04453b8765df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8debb9f-1a0f-5757-8884-2cca273ae3ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-10871" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5} & =0 \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(16,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142b6536-fed4-529f-ac1d-8a42fc666730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244498} {:answer/id #uuid "65112d5b-e5af-58b2-8b42-2fafe84f3bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245208} {:answer/id #uuid "6e29c4b1-5a97-5a97-bbf2-51eabed39e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245289} {:answer/id #uuid "ca93f7a3-eace-5053-ae98-706828a1b4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246111} {:answer/id #uuid "d23fbfd4-1cbd-54d1-aa24-886860426aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23fbfd4-1cbd-54d1-aa24-886860426aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246184} :answer-field/id #uuid "816b14ca-c9ce-5b90-8b9c-06306f5bacc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246773}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24c7ef87-44ff-5aaa-8c80-717e24087d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-11124" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+2 \\\\ y & =-x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0+3 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233120 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73d11496-6181-52ca-9d4d-b27d18c44afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-10878" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}$ at the point $(3,9)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(3)=2(3)=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{6}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}+9 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{19}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}x \\\\ x & =57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(57,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "cbb07659-6477-5d10-82e6-aacdbe268de0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232870} {:answer-field/id #uuid "8c274c86-2265-560b-954c-b88302a2ca2b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232871}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fa5d441-4b5d-54d5-8541-9752428a4a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-342222" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt[4]{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt[4]{x}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{1}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-2\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y & =-2x+2+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,2,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234308 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5576e6ce-2a23-51d7-abb9-62b7ddb98d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-71542" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-4x+1$ at the point $(2,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)=6x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)-4=8,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{4}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\frac{21}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5ae20c-b789-5249-bff8-1194daf3c008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238321} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc902fd-c7c3-53b3-90a3-02661fb40990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c92a83-db3b-57d5-87ad-2844ddc86d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239896} {:answer/id #uuid "ee996a51-be22-5564-9782-5ded740f8f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240158} {:answer/id #uuid "f14cea86-7dde-5527-ac9c-2767c9873574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc902fd-c7c3-53b3-90a3-02661fb40990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239711} :answer-field/id #uuid "092d888b-3d25-51dd-8dad-08178716d6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9348de04-831f-5ba3-963b-71f2ffb24276" :question/math-academy-id "q-104073" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(8,12)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-12 & =-(x-8) \\\\ y & =-x+8+12 \\\\ y & =20-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20-0 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,20).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aec5b68-893e-5d74-9a97-1f1d78197fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238434} {:answer/id #uuid "39be9afa-fb3a-514b-a31f-626575d486e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{7},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238553} {:answer/id #uuid "659ab06c-c705-55e3-b1cd-020841118552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238943} {:answer/id #uuid "81f08a0b-8c19-5ecf-80ec-e6d88e15c8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239184} {:answer/id #uuid "a3667d4f-7c52-56e0-aa34-3236afb88174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3667d4f-7c52-56e0-aa34-3236afb88174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239478} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02d54c7-4347-5d6c-bb82-af5e4a1dca44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240752}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4c51a1e-c57a-5c02-97b1-4b0a0b84934b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104075" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3\\sqrt{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at  $(7,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c44b5185-3568-53b3-8d77-c69fc2155067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c44b5185-3568-53b3-8d77-c69fc2155067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296303} :answer-field/id #uuid "26be343c-5dfd-5031-b3d8-1563d7798319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55838d61-7b48-5ee6-9d1d-e85a07554987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55838d61-7b48-5ee6-9d1d-e85a07554987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295760} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48c190e-f08c-555e-ba42-c4bbbf078a30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297131}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac03aacc-0b7e-5a7f-8554-ee3b669a879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-308823" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+1$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{(1)^{2}}=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f29a7ee8-6004-5b63-95be-61dae16a9261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f29a7ee8-6004-5b63-95be-61dae16a9261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37137d9-0f3c-517e-a020-3af32e0d7118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956a031d-532f-5607-903a-8423c21b005b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956a031d-532f-5607-903a-8423c21b005b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3b85e2-94cd-5534-8e57-152046c2fe00" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13b0b6f2-9e09-5a5a-a8f6-564d0221d479" :question/math-academy-id "q-342200" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-2(1)=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb0d502b-d760-5a8c-9578-c9ee4ec4fcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0d502b-d760-5a8c-9578-c9ee4ec4fcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316175} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1ebef9-6d7b-5c01-bb3d-23418a788f7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20941452-3ae7-5d6b-8a37-42ad9a21730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20941452-3ae7-5d6b-8a37-42ad9a21730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315301} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf70354-1c4a-52c1-8859-6faef41810f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317077}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f7f3a21-7e8d-51e9-8d45-2915b0e3fe06" :question/math-academy-id "q-342201" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-6x+5$ at the point $(0,5)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+5)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=6(0)-6=-6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-6)}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x+5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-30,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028288df-333f-57f2-9e25-7548b283399c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493307} {:answer/id #uuid "30a55d4b-f895-53a5-a364-ce5fddbb6ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493310} {:answer/id #uuid "59d2a5db-49a2-566c-afa9-b7bdaafa25da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{376}{25},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493313} {:answer/id #uuid "66291432-b739-52c2-992c-c3d5906e8d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(376,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493314} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3d8d25-8bfa-5e2a-b032-69c3782c0eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(25,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66291432-b739-52c2-992c-c3d5906e8d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(376,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493314} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ab1d54-64ea-58e4-9d91-d527f34b3bee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e3f1a4a-bfde-54af-80e2-4cc4e890e6fc" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=10 x^{2} + 5 x$ at the point $(1,15)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(10 x^{2} + 5 x)=20 x + 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1)=25=25,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(25)}=- \\frac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(15)&=- \\frac{1}{25}(x-(1))\\\\y&=- \\frac{1}{25}x-(- \\frac{1}{25})+(15)\\\\y&=- \\frac{1}{25}x+\\frac{376}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{25}x+\\frac{376}{25}&=0\\\\x&=376.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(376,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4079920d-ee6c-5086-9aa8-97eb49b915c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4079920d-ee6c-5086-9aa8-97eb49b915c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493311} :answer-field/id #uuid "24d2eda6-5f52-58d1-8821-c19db30c5bf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9c2120-ea78-56ae-b7da-fc8f913f4ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9c2120-ea78-56ae-b7da-fc8f913f4ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493320} :answer-field/id #uuid "a11eaf98-7e1d-530f-93f1-50c6c3aa85a7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c1268dd-9a0b-5590-983e-596506fe3286" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{20}{x}+10$ at the point $(1,30)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(\\frac{20}{x}+10)\\\\&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(10 + \\frac{20}{x})\\\\&=-20x^{-2}\\\\&=- \\frac{20}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1)=-20=-20,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-20)}=\\frac{1}{20}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(30)&=\\frac{1}{20}(x-(1))\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{20}x-(\\frac{1}{20})+(30)\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{20}x+\\frac{599}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}x+\\frac{599}{20}&=0\\\\x&=-599.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(-599,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93fb4c05-1e1a-5463-aeeb-5bdc06ba8f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93fb4c05-1e1a-5463-aeeb-5bdc06ba8f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1080" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493317} :answer-field/id #uuid "7468eab7-aa1d-581c-b1c7-75e4eb6144f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493315} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17fbaf02-25c3-5f36-8455-1d21e7ade415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17fbaf02-25c3-5f36-8455-1d21e7ade415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493308} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f48b25-30b8-5af5-8b60-15f91577b14d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493323}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3c9763c-f1e1-51a2-887f-38a4a610161d" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=18 x^{2} - 36 x + 30$ at the point $(0,30)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(18 x^{2} - 36 x + 30)=36 x - 36.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(0)=-36=-36,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-36)}=\\frac{1}{36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(30)&=\\frac{1}{36}(x-(0))\\\\y&=\\frac{1}{36}x+30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{36}x+30&=0\\\\\\frac{1}{36}x&=-30\\\\x&=-1080.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(-1080,0).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096403 :question/id #uuid "74ffb6be-e709-5635-b6e2-98911c40a19e" :question/math-academy-id "e-184" :question/problem "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2^x +1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t&= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t        \t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot  \\left( 2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{e^{3x}  \\left( 3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2}\n\t\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/math-academy-id 1110 :topic/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a5acaf-0b97-5eec-b5dc-80f14127936e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251028} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc87138-62df-5eac-9d34-752a109148de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{(3x+5)}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251338} {:answer/id #uuid "794835b2-c8d2-5e60-99aa-d141cf6639aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251559} {:answer/id #uuid "80538af1-96e8-546c-8e04-53ab4e77cd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{(3x+5)}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251634} {:answer/id #uuid "e543d792-894d-58ae-966d-434f788becaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{{(3x+5)}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "794835b2-c8d2-5e60-99aa-d141cf6639aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251559} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc00428-34a0-5c80-bcd3-256eb4ce28ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4603accf-ccdb-5030-90b8-c9b568c4df0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11973" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(3x+5)^{2}}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x){(3x+5)}^{2}-(3{x}^{2})\\cdot 6(3x+5)}{{((3x+5)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x(3x+5)(3x+5-3x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x(3x+5)(5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91221116-88ce-521b-bad3-726d8605d47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289006} {:answer/id #uuid "cce2b4d8-a4ef-58c6-9b31-914df56eedb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{16{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289395} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b28c22-8699-5534-b552-2ffbcb24e1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289479} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7cddbb-8584-5d98-9e76-f760c3dad214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{16{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289591} {:answer/id #uuid "f7dc46cb-df18-5bde-895e-17fa801c55f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91221116-88ce-521b-bad3-726d8605d47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289006} :answer-field/id #uuid "379f9fa9-539c-5ea4-aa1b-2d627f7a6c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289853}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cae07ba-0147-5b66-85db-519e500d15f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-2396" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)$ and the denominator $v(x)=4x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2))(4x)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{⧸}4(7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2))}{\\text{⧸}4(4{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7x{sec}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x-2)}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233662 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8304b514-754e-54d2-b053-31a4e39c33dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-2400" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{{x}^{3}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=\\frac{3}{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{x})({x}^{3}+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3}))(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+2)-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f3cad9e-3847-5b2c-9a04-8780ba7d30c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f3cad9e-3847-5b2c-9a04-8780ba7d30c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232545} :answer-field/id #uuid "4246690e-8a0c-5ab6-84d7-36700b1fc9a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232544}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c35d38fd-8b5e-5d85-92e9-92885a61eb77" :question/math-academy-id "q-313362" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(3x+5)^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(3x+5)}^{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}){(3x+5)}^{2}-({x}^{3})(6(3x+5))}{{({(3x+5)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}{(3x+5)}^{2}-6{x}^{3}(3x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(3x+5)((3x+5)-2x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cffe24-94be-52e7-a238-f2cdd9eed192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0cffe24-94be-52e7-a238-f2cdd9eed192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234843} :answer-field/id #uuid "9adce06b-fd56-5896-83e4-f0d2d7a527b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8c9449b-c7f6-57bf-a86b-68a3efa54686" :question/math-academy-id "q-128660" :question/problem "Consider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{21}{25}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{7}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3}=-\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5-25f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+3.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+3)-(5-25f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot (g(2)+3)-(5-25f(2))\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2)+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\cdot (-\\frac{21}{25})\\cdot (-2+3)-(5-25\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-2+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =21-20 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2ffda4-7650-5f8b-b0fe-b79f26714743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e2ffda4-7650-5f8b-b0fe-b79f26714743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234619} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3d02e6-9dc2-5595-90e5-be19d90fb6ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235051}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "295b1f08-4feb-5011-b27e-88e7956a72df" :question/math-academy-id "q-132975" :question/problem "${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=2t$ and the denominator $h(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=2,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(2t)\\cdot (8t(2{t}^{2}+3))}{{((2{t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-16{t}^{2}(2{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}+6-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010ec989-6a70-5560-8c6d-96bcabcf07f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250561} {:answer/id #uuid "185de746-f2a2-5bc0-a895-8eef91887f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(x+1)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250767} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf0aeab-facc-582b-9a39-b2c196aead5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251320} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f44c2b-b5c6-5adc-a37b-c3386048fc4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-x)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252297} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b8be0a-88c7-5856-ade3-8586c358ec8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf0aeab-facc-582b-9a39-b2c196aead5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251320} :answer-field/id #uuid "910e876b-71ca-514d-ad43-ceb4c9229334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253056}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3000a138-321d-511f-bb2f-c25bd88c5e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-11978" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(x+1))({x}^{2}+1)-((x+1)^{2})(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)-2x(x+1)^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(({x}^{2}+1)-x(x+1))}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{2}-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(1-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018421ea-dca9-5daf-ab3a-c27696ce4249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288034} {:answer/id #uuid "1e02db35-b595-59ea-973e-bd157371128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288223} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5c0bc2-0072-5944-8dd3-fe8dcbae8938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288693} {:answer/id #uuid "ac81cfb0-7259-5f57-9adb-04296e897ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289177} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7a7d1a-20e3-5bd6-9408-f184d8d4065a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac81cfb0-7259-5f57-9adb-04296e897ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45efbdd-39dd-5ca7-b621-baab9c7920f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290238}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3d7d091-4a85-557a-b384-1cb4715f620a" :question/math-academy-id "q-2403" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}})(2x)-(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})(2)}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157a2036-1879-59ee-a825-db1e218bddff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "157a2036-1879-59ee-a825-db1e218bddff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295394} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e445d7b-7788-5470-b989-fa47d0509c5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296828}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07a6d700-2f06-5564-a6ab-09de601041cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-313365" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(x+3)}^{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+3.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)\\cdot (x+3)-(x+1)^{2}\\cdot 1}{(x+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)[2(x+3)-(x+1)]}{{(x+3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)[2x+6-x-1]}{{(x+3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+5)}{{(x+3)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bedcd8f-d90b-5061-91ac-f10991c0035c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bedcd8f-d90b-5061-91ac-f10991c0035c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2x\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295522} :answer-field/id #uuid "441576ee-9428-5996-8d80-7db6b8216c3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296762}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8e96d3e-9fca-5093-8b9b-5bb4483b8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-313371" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x{e}^{2x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the denominator $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})({e}^{2x})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a36b37d-bc34-5c1e-abe5-aba0572d9529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a36b37d-bc34-5c1e-abe5-aba0572d9529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315359} :answer-field/id #uuid "4561e01a-704e-54aa-a836-dee4e008d320" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316704}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1cf5087-7d07-5a4d-8585-5e5e761a770e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343546" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{x}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and the denominator $v(x)=x.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-1)-0}{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3102034-c50a-54cf-a329-ded3453fa915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3102034-c50a-54cf-a329-ded3453fa915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316473} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4e8ab4-162d-564a-9ba4-54a72694dc88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2ff9150-55a2-5862-9d62-f9ca9a7361d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343576" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{x+2},$ find ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n${y}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})(x+2)-{e}^{3x}(1)}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+6-1)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+5)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of ${y}^{′}(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{(3(1)+5)({e}^{3(1)})}{((1)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cd8b93-2f43-5245-9465-c6f50585f13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}-12x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65cd8b93-2f43-5245-9465-c6f50585f13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}-12x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315683} :answer-field/id #uuid "33865aab-8dfb-55db-a97b-6ae9377bd664" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316665}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67995b3a-2ea5-5ccb-abc6-62f10fe0e1da" :question/math-academy-id "q-344143" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\frac{{e}^{3{x}^{2}}}{x-2}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | $(${{field-1}}$){e}^{3{x}^{2}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-2)^{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3{x}^{2}}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x-2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}})(x-2)-({e}^{3{x}^{2}})(1)}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3{x}^{2}}(6x(x-2)-1)}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6{x}^{2}-12x-1){e}^{3{x}^{2}}}{(x-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3ff14c-bd71-5edd-9ce3-7a15e330b984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3ff14c-bd71-5edd-9ce3-7a15e330b984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487462} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbbff3c0-fd43-59f0-8b75-8d882b1ddde3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487467}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06dba1a6-575d-58cc-96f9-bc295e858086" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=e^{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(e^{x})}{(e^{x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7eed3b-6e94-58ff-a735-b8d7429d5a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7eed3b-6e94-58ff-a735-b8d7429d5a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487463} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ec5f5b-1856-5a8b-a828-90b43d0da5df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487458}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bad9e9f-685f-504c-b1b2-46b994dcd8dd" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{9 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{9 x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{9 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=9 e^{9 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{9 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(9 e^{9 x})}{(e^{9 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(9 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 9 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da5edfb5-96f8-5e7d-89f2-9f743bf0bb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da5edfb5-96f8-5e7d-89f2-9f743bf0bb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487466} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9ca27b6-6a71-5e3d-abb9-6924714b214c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43481be6-03be-562b-89a5-7c8695090e89" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{3 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{3 x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{3 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=3 e^{3 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{3 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(3 e^{3 x})}{(e^{3 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 3 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ef752fe-c8e7-5714-85a9-0630780ed5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef752fe-c8e7-5714-85a9-0630780ed5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487461} :answer-field/id #uuid "be083d33-9396-5f2d-8f6c-2365e427f22c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be93be3a-f700-523a-abbb-8f17fed03030" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{4 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{4 x}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{4 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=4 e^{4 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{4 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(4 e^{4 x})}{(e^{4 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 4 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}]}
